summary:
PARTIAL after round 8. e274cc81d makes the scanner skip all three bytes of backslash-CR-LF inside string and character literals, with planted rows (CRLF string, the LF control, CRLF character literal, a CRLF-continued literal that only mentions the name, a CRLF-continued line comment); the commit message reports that reverting the fix fails 3 assertions and that the real tree is byte-identical to the previous lexer. ASTRA (narrow confirmation, 32bf2462e) CONFIRMED the ORIGINAL defect fixed: his original CRLF string probe fails against the old lexer and passes against the new one, the character-literal form passes, and direct `//` CRLF continuation, CRLF raw strings, mixed LF/CRLF continuations and a file ending in a backslash passed his probes. NOT CLOSED: he demonstrated two further compilable spellings that still hide a following call at 32bf2462e (g++ 15.2 -fsyntax-only, pinned lexer bodies with constexpr added): (a) TWO backslashes immediately before CRLF inside a literal (a character literal of quote, backslash, backslash, CR, LF, n, quote splices to '
', but Tripwire:205 skips the pair, stops at LF and blanks the call; a Windows-path string split the same way reproduces it); (b) WHITESPACE after the continuation backslash (b"// note \ 
 /* example
m.submit_bound();
/* close */
" compiles but the call disappears, because Tripwire:154 requires the backslash immediately before CRLF; a continued ordinary string with a space after its backslash also fails). Derived facts (Astra): a contributor-authored continuation triggers a demonstrated false negative in the required activation gate; I6(b) HIGH, no E1/E2 escalation, no E6 cap; the lexical outcome is experimentally demonstrated. He refuses to adjudicate the row until these are fixed and regression-tested.
